    "summary": "Textile (perhaps a baby-tie or a sash), aso oke, composed of four strips of imported European cotton in two shades of brown, which have been hand-woven and hand-sewn together along the selvedges. The warp is formed of alternating narrow stripes of either shade of brown. A complicated pattern is created in the weft using three decorative weaving techniques: alternating the ground weft between the two shades of brown; adding bundles of threads of supplementary weft; and creating circular or oval holes by tying warp threads together and carrying the lines of twisted warp threads from one hole to the next (‘aso eleya’: the last two techniques are specific to the Yoruba). The textile has been hemmed by hand.",
